## 3. Lobby / room pattern

**One DO per room code.** Route `/room/:code/ws`; the Hono entry derives a stub from the code and forwards the raw upgrade request to the DO. Use the modern `getByName` API (returns a stub; call `.fetch(request)` for the upgrade).

```ts
// src/index.ts  (Hono entry — app.fetch is the Worker fetch handler)
import { Hono } from "hono";
import { RoomDurableObject } from "./room-do";

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Env }>();

// static files are served by [assets] without hitting Hono; WS + API go through here.
app.get("/room/:code/ws", (c) => {
  const upgrade = c.req.header("Upgrade");
  if (upgrade !== "websocket") return c.text("Expected websocket", 426);
  const code = c.req.param("code").toUpperCase();
  const stub = c.env.ROOMS.getByName(code);   // one DO instance per room code
  return stub.fetch(c.req.raw);                 // DO does WebSocketPair + acceptWebSocket
});

// optional: REST for lobby metadata, create code, etc.
app.get("/api/health", (c) => c.json({ ok: true }));

export default app; // Hono's app.fetch is the module fetch handler
```

`Env` type:
```ts
interface Env {
  ROOMS: DurableObjectNamespace<RoomDurableObject>;
  ASSETS: Fetcher;
}
```

(Use `wrangler types --env-interface CloudflareBindings` to generate this from wrangler.jsonc.)

**Broadcast at 10–15 Hz — client-driven, not server-ticked.** Each client sends its position ~10–15×/s; the DO relays to other sockets in `webSocketMessage`. This is the cost-optimal pattern: incoming messages keep the DO alive during play, and when nobody moves the DO **hibernates** (≈0 duration cost). Avoid a server-side `setInterval` game loop — it pins the DO in memory and defeats hibernation. For Wobble Rush (physics authoritative client-side + relay), this is the right model.

**Idle cleanup / lifecycle** — two complementary mechanisms:

1. **Hibernation (automatic):** an empty/idle room hibernates on its own; no code needed. You pay only for the WS connection metadata while hibernated, not GB-s duration.

2. **Alarms for TTL / final teardown.** One alarm per DO (`ctx.storage.setAlarm(ms)`, `getAlarm()`, `deleteAlarm()`, `alarm()` handler — guaranteed at-least-once, retried with exponential backoff). The documented TTL pattern:
```ts
async fetch(request: Request) {
  // extend TTL on every request
  if (!await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) {        // don't clobber an existing alarm
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 5 * 60_000);  // 5 min idle
  }
  // ...handle upgrade...
  return ...;
}
async alarm() {
  if (this.ctx.getWebSockets().length === 0) {
    await this.ctx.storage.deleteAll();           // tear down empty room
  } else {
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 5 * 60_000); // re-arm while populated
  }
}
```
**Do not** call `setAlarm` unconditionally in the constructor — the constructor runs *before* `alarm()` on a wake, so a naive constructor would keep extending the TTL and starve the alarm handler. Always guard with `getAlarm()` first.

**Cost notes:** Hibernation = no GB-s duration charges while idle; you pay for WS messages (requests), active duration while a room is playing, and SQLite storage. SQLite-backed DOs also get Point-in-Time Recovery (30 days). DOs scale horizontally by room code (one object per code), with a soft ~1,000 req/s limit per individual object — fine for a party racer.

Docs:
- DO get-started (`getByName`): https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/get-started/
- Alarms API: https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/
- DO Time To Live example: https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/examples/durable-object-ttl/
- Hono on Cloudflare Workers: https://hono.dev/docs/getting-started/cloudflare-workers

